## Series context & the key point _(why this concept, not a generic one)_

This is the **foundation article for the entire Sleep series**, and its whole job is to install one mental object the reader has never actually pictured: **the shape of a night.** Not "how much sleep" (a number), but **how a night is built** — and why that shape, not the total, decides what you get out of it.

The article makes four moves the poster must carry:

1. **Sleep is not "off."** It's an active, staged process. (The "closing the shop to do the work you can't do with customers in the building" image.)
2. **Four stages, in ~90-minute cycles** — N1, N2, N3 (deep / slow-wave), REM.
3. **THE central insight — the architecture is asymmetric.** Deep sleep (N3) **front-loads** the night (the first ~3 hours = the body's physical-repair window: the big GH pulse, tissue repair, glymphatic brain-clearance). REM **back-loads** it (the last ~3 hours = the brain's window: emotional regulation, memory integration, creativity). A night is a **gradient**, deep at the front, REM at the back — *not a stack of identical blocks.*
4. **The consequence that makes it matter — you cannot cherry-pick.** Cutting a night short does **not** trim sleep evenly; it **amputates a specific stage.** Bed at 2 AM instead of 11 PM (same 7 AM wake) keeps most deep sleep but **destroys REM.** A 6-hour night is not 75% of an 8-hour night — depending on *which* 6 hours, you keep 90% of one job and 40% of another.

The thesis that frames all five articles: **==You don't take sleep, you build it.==** You cannot force sleep; you can only build the conditions under which the brain *allows* it. Everything downstream (the cold room, the caffeine cutoff, the melatonin myth, the DORA reset) is just **defending the architecture** — which is exactly why the architecture has to be drawn first.

**The single thing a reader struggles to visualize and must walk away owning:** *A night has a shape. It runs deep early (the body repairs) and dreamy late (the brain repairs). Lose sleep off the front and you rob your body; lose it off the back — the usual way, by waking early or going to bed late — and you rob your brain. The total hours lie to you; the shape tells the truth.*

That is a **cross-sectional, asymmetric, depth-vs-time object** — which is why the only honest anchor is a **hypnogram rendered as literal architecture.** The article hands us the word "architecture" on purpose. We take it literally.

