## 1. The Big Idea

_One sentence: **A night is not a flat block of "sleep" — it's a building with a shape, deep-loaded at the front (where the body is repaired) and REM-loaded at the back (where the brain is repaired), and the way you lose sleep decides which floor you demolish.** The poster makes the reader feel, instantly, that "I slept 6 hours" is a meaningless number until you know *which* 6 hours — because the night's architecture is asymmetric, and cutting it short is an amputation, not a trim._

## 2. Visual Metaphor & Structure — the hero object

**A hypnogram drawn as the cross-section of a building at night.** This is the anchor; nothing replaces it.

- **Horizontal axis = TIME**, left to right: `LIGHTS OUT` → `WAKE`, ~8 hours, with faint hour ticks (11 PM … 7 AM) and four-to-six soft **cycle** divisions (~90 min each).
- **Vertical axis = DEPTH**, top to bottom: a labeled left-edge ruler reading (top→bottom) `AWAKE · N1 · N2 · N3 (DEEP)`, with **REM** sitting as a distinct band near the top (brain wake-like, body paralysed — so it reads "high" on the depth axis but is colored as its own thing, never confused with being awake).
- **The trace itself = the real shape of a healthy night:** the classic sawtooth that **plunges deep fast in the first two cycles** (big N3 troughs at the front), then **the troughs get shallower each cycle while the REM bands at the top grow longer toward morning.** The asymmetry must be visually obvious at a glance: *deep wedges crowd the left, REM ribbons stretch across the right.*
- **The architecture overlay (this is what makes it a "building," not just a chart):** treat the area under the curve as a **cross-sectioned structure.**
  - The **front third** (deep-rich) is the **foundation / basement / load-bearing level** — warm, structural, captioned `THE BODY · physical repair`. Dock the deep-sleep jobs here as small etched glyphs: **GH pulse** (~70% of daily output, minutes after you fall asleep), **tissue repair**, **glymphatic clearance** (the brain "takes out its trash"), **immune work**.
  - The **back third** (REM-rich) is the **upper floors** — cool, lighter, captioned `THE BRAIN · emotional & cognitive repair`. Dock the REM jobs here: **memory integration**, **emotional recalibration** (takes the *charge* off the day), **creativity / "sleep on it."**
  - The **middle** is N2 connective structure — the bulk of the night, the "stairs between floors."

**The amputation gesture (the emotional payload).** Overlay a **vertical guillotine line** with two positions and a one-line consequence each:

- A red cut on the **right** labeled `WAKE 2 HRS EARLY` (early alarm / early flight) → the slice it removes is the **REM-rich upper floors** → caption: `you keep the body, you lose the brain.`
- A red cut on the **left/start** labeled `TO BED AT 2 AM` (same wake time) → it **compresses the deep front** → caption: `the night that lies: same "hours," different building.`
- The headline math sits beside the cuts: **`A 6-hour night ≠ 75% of an 8-hour night.`**

Why this metaphor fits and nothing else does: the article's literal claim is that sleep has an *architecture* — asymmetric, layered, front-to-back ordered, and destroyed unevenly when shortened. A pyramid, a dial, or a pie chart all flatten exactly the asymmetry that is the point. A depth-vs-time cross-section is the *only* shape that is simultaneously the real hypnogram (scientifically honest) and a building you can "cut" (emotionally legible).

## 3. Layout Description

**Portrait (poster / blog-hero, ~4:5 or 3:4)** so the depth axis has room to breathe and the stage ruler can sit tall on the left. (A landscape variant works for an in-article banner; portrait is primary.)

- **Top band (~12%):** the headline (see §4), small, set against the night sky, kerned wide. A thin horizon line under it.
- **Center stage (~62%) — the hero:** the hypnogram-cross-section fills the full width. The eye enters top-left at `LIGHTS OUT`, is pulled **down** into the deep front wedges (the warm foundation), then **rides the sawtooth rightward and upward** as the troughs shallow and the REM ribbons lengthen — so the reader's gaze physically traces the night from body-repair to brain-repair. The two red guillotine cuts sit over the trace, restrained, like surveyor marks.
- **Foundation strip (~14%), directly under the trace:** a two-room "cutaway floorplan" — left room `THE BODY` (deep-sleep jobs), right room `THE BRAIN` (REM jobs) — visually bolted to the underside of the curve so it reads as the building's program, not a separate legend.
- **Bottom band (~12%):** the thesis lockup `YOU DON'T TAKE SLEEP — YOU BUILD IT`, plus a thin footer: `Part 1 of 5 · Sleep · the Healthy pillar` and the "everything after this just defends the architecture" micro-caption.

Reading path is deliberate and matches the argument: **down into the body → across and up into the brain → the cut lines that threaten both → the thesis that resolves it.**

## 4. Text Elements _(keep minimal — every word earns its place)_

- **Headline (pick one):**
  - `A NIGHT HAS A SHAPE.` (cleanest, most poster-like)
  - `THE ARCHITECTURE OF A NIGHT` (titular, safe)
  - `"I SLEPT 6 HOURS" TELLS YOU NOTHING.` (provocation variant — strongest scroll-stopper)
- **Axis labels:** left ruler `AWAKE · REM · N1 · N2 · N3 DEEP`; baseline `LIGHTS OUT → WAKE` with hour ticks.
- **Two-half captions on the trace:** `← THE BODY (deep sleep · front of night)` and `THE BRAIN (REM · back of night) →`.
- **The math callout:** `6 hours ≠ 75% of 8.` (sub-line, tiny: `cut the night and you amputate a stage, not a slice.`)
- **Two cut labels:** `WAKE EARLY → lose REM (the brain)` · `LATE TO BED → compress deep (the body)`.
- **Foundation-room labels:** under THE BODY → `GH pulse · tissue repair · brain trash-out · immunity`; under THE BRAIN → `memory · emotion · creativity`.
- **Thesis lockup (bottom):** `YOU DON'T TAKE SLEEP. YOU BUILD IT.`

Nothing else. No bullet lists of "tips," no paragraph of body copy — the chart *is* the argument.

## 5. Color & Mood

**Mood:** nocturnal, calm, and quietly serious — a planetarium-meets-medical-chart register. This is not motivational and not alarmist; it's the feeling of being shown the blueprint of something you do every night and never saw. Clinical precision wrapped in night.

**Palette (dark-first):**
- **Background:** deep near-black indigo / ink (`#0B0F1A`-ish) — the night, not pure black; a barely-there top-to-bottom gradient (slightly lighter "sky" at top, darker "ground" at the foundation).
- **Accent 1 — THE BODY / deep sleep:** a warm **amber / ember** (`#E8A23D`-ish). Warm = restorative, physical, "the furnace running in the basement." Used for the deep front wedges and the BODY room.
- **Accent 2 — THE BRAIN / REM:** a cool **violet / periwinkle** (`#8B7CF0`-ish). Cool, dreamlike, electric. Used for the REM ribbons and the BRAIN room.
- **Structure / text / ruler:** soft off-white (`#E6E8EE`) and a muted slate for secondary labels — never harsh pure white.
- **One alarm color — the cuts only:** a restrained **signal red / coral** (`#FF5C5C`), used *exclusively* for the two guillotine lines and the `6 ≠ 75%` math. Scarcity is what gives it force.

The warm-front / cool-back split does double duty: it's beautiful *and* it encodes the body→brain gradient, so the color itself teaches the asymmetry before a single word is read.

## 6. Style Reference

**"Clinical nocturne" — a scientific diagram with restraint and atmosphere.** Touchstones:

- The information honesty and labeled-axis discipline of a **real sleep-lab hypnogram / polysomnograph readout** (this keeps it credible, not decorative).
- The dark, layered, cross-section elegance of **editorial science graphics** (think *Quanta* / *Scientific American* full-page diagrams, or a Wait-But-Why concept rendered for grown-ups and at night).
- A touch of **architectural cross-section / blueprint** language for the foundation strip (thin etched lines, room labels, a sense of "cutaway").

It should sit as a **sibling** to the Fit pillar's "Engineering Diagnostic Instrument" style — same intellectual seriousness, same instrument-grade labeling and single-alarm-color discipline — but where Fit is a daylit machine on a workbench, **Sleep is the same rigor rendered after dark.** This establishes the visual signature for the rest of the 5-part Sleep series (Parts 2.0–3.1 can reuse the night palette, the warm/cool body-brain coding, and the red-for-warnings rule).

## 7. What to Avoid

- **The cliché of a person asleep in bed with floating Zzz's / a crescent moon / a glowing alarm clock.** It says "sleep" and teaches nothing. The whole point is the *invisible internal shape*, not the visible sleeper — never show a bed or a face.
- **A flat, symmetric chart — a pie of "20% REM / 25% deep…", evenly-stacked bars, or a tidy four-quadrant grid.** These destroy the one idea worth keeping: the **asymmetry** (deep front, REM back) and the fact that loss is **uneven**. If the graphic could be redrawn as a balanced pie without losing information, it has failed the article.
- (Bonus to avoid: making the REM band sit *above* "AWAKE" or look like wakefulness — REM is sleep; color it as its own thing so no reader thinks the dreaming hours are "almost awake = wasted.")
